<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.9.2: Analyze Underlying Relationship in a Graph of a Function</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01405</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.9.2: Analyze Underlying Relationship in a Graph of a Function</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2274ad70-5eec-4c13-8e6d-b129d7b28eb8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title> Activity</title>
<para>The cost, in dollars, to produce 1 watt of solar energy is a function of the number of years since 1977, \(t\).</para>
<para>From 1977 to 1987, the cost could be modeled by an exponential function \(f\). Here is the graph of the function.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="A sheet of solar panels."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/fc18f868b9d9768cc0ff232f4b608bfb26081391"/></media></figure></para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a0f79653ec7d1fa67449d4d8872e608bcbc6e9a2"/></media></figure></para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the statement \(f(9) \approx 6\) saying about this situation?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> 9 years after 1977 (or in 1986), the cost in dollars for one watt of solar energy was about $6.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What is \(f(4)\)? What about \(f(3.5)\)? What do these values represent in this context?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(f(4) \approx 25\), and \(f(3.5) \approx 30\). These values represent the cost in dollars of 1 watt of solar energy in 1981 (4 years after 1977) and halfway through 1980 (3.5 years after 1977).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> When \(f(t)=45\), what is \(t\)? What does that value of \(t\) represent in this context?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>2 years after 1977 (in 1979), the cost in dollars of 1 watt of solar energy was $45.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>By what factor did the cost of 1 watt of solar energy change each year? (If you get stuck, consider creating a table.)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(\frac34\) or 0.75 </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<title>Video: Understanding Characteristics of Graphs</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about the characteristics of graphs: </para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <div class="os-raise-video-container"><video controls="true" crossorigin="anonymous">
